#  Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in a working tree, on every
#  host.
#
#  Not a preference. The conformance suite compares what a program printed
#  against text kept here, the repository checks read this repository's own
#  files, and a case reads a file it just wrote -- all of which compare bytes.
#  A checkout that turned LF into CR LF on Windows made 374 message keys
#  "missing" from a catalog that had every one of them, because the locale read
#  out of it carried a carriage return.
* text=auto eol=lf

#  Recorded output of the examples: byte-for-byte what the shell printed.
*.expected -text

#  The two fixtures whose whole point is their line endings: a script as a
#  Windows editor writes one. Held at CR LF everywhere, so the case that says a
#  shell reads such a file is asserting that on every host rather than only on
#  the one whose checkout happened to produce it.
conformance/fixtures/crlf.adash -text
conformance/fixtures/crlf-faulty.adash -text
